 "source_url": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=r4VmnuVAqoM",
 "title": "Handling Cold Call Objections: In a Meeting",
 "topic": "sales",
 "summary": "The creator claims that when a prospect says they are in a meeting during a cold call, it is usually a smoke screen or a lie because people typically do not pick up random calls during important meetings. The lesson is to test for intent and confirm a specific time before calling them back.",
 "key_points": [
  "Treat the 'I'm in a meeting, call me later' objection as a smoke screen or a lie since nobody in a real meeting answers a random call.",
  "Check for intent and confirm a specific time before calling them back.",
  "Use this script before letting them go: 'Hey, listen, before I let you go and call you back, I just want to know, is this something you're even interested in? If you're not, I don't want to waste your time.'",
  "Follow the follow-up protocol after checking intent."
 ],
 "content": "The creator discusses how to handle a cold call objection where a prospect says, \"I'm in a very important meeting, call me later!\" The creator argues that if someone is genuinely in an important meeting, they would not pick up their phone for a random call. Therefore, \"That's a smoke screen\" and a lie. Since they did pick up the call, the creator advises to \"check for intent and confirm time before you call them back.\" The creator provides the exact lines to say: \"Hey, listen, before I let you go and call you back, I just want to know, is this something you're even interested in? If you're not, I don't want to waste your time.\" After checking intent, you can proceed with the follow-up protocol.",
